And you had a choice to make, I would imagine.
我想你当时肯定面临着一个选择。

00:18:13.809 --> 00:18:26.450
You had a choice to make of how much to reflect your life on this screen, we'll talk about potential future screens, but on this screen and what went through your head?
你面临着一个选择，即要在屏幕上折射多少你的生活——我们稍后会谈论未来潜在的屏幕呈现——但在这块屏幕上，你当时脑子里在想什么？

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:30.890
Yeah, when we got up to that point in the series, I mean, it was a few years later, right?
是的，当我们推进到剧集的那个阶段时，我的意思是，那是几年后了，对吧？

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:39.170
it was like, I'm forgetting to remember what season it was, but at this point, my son would have been four or five or whatever it was.
就像是，我有点记不清是第几季了，但在那个时候，我儿子应该四五岁或者差不多大吧。

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:48.049
And I remember having this real soul search of these characters that are ostensibly based on my wife and I, Marshall and Lily.
我记得当时内心进行了真正的灵魂拷问：这些表面上以我和我妻子为原型的角色，马歇尔和莉莉。

00:18:48.049 --> 00:18:52.269
Do we write it into the show?
我们要把它写进剧里吗？

00:18:52.269 --> 00:18:57.869
Do we write them having a kid who is in some way complicated medically, developmentally, whatever it was gonna be?
我们要写他们生了一个在医疗、发育或其他方面有些复杂的小孩吗？

00:18:57.869 --> 00:19:00.150
Can I do that?
我能做到这一点吗？

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:01.829
Can we, does the audience want to see that?
我们能吗，观众想看到这样的内容吗？

00:19:01.829 --> 00:19:03.670
Will the network allow us to do that?
电视台会允许我们这么做吗？

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:05.509
Is it, can we even consider doing that?
这是……我们甚至能考虑这样做吗？

00:19:05.509 --> 00:19:10.069
That's not what the show is really about, but can we make it be in part about that?
这本来不是这部剧的核心内容，但我们能让它部分地关乎这个吗？

00:19:10.069 --> 00:19:17.630
And I remember having some real soul searching about that and continue to feel mixed about it to this day that in the end I decided no.
我记得当时对此进行了一些深刻的自我反省，直到今天我对这件事的感受依然很复杂，因为我最后决定不这么做。

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:24.509
I think we might have floated it to the studio and network and I don't remember them being super crazy about the idea.
我想我们可能向制片厂和电视台稍微透露过这个想法，但我不记得他们对这个主意有多么热衷。

00:19:24.509 --> 00:19:31.238
Carter Bayes was wonderfully supportive and was like, look, we can figure out a way to talk about this on the show.
卡特·贝斯非常支持，并表示，听着，我们可以想办法在剧里讨论这个。

00:19:31.238 --> 00:19:32.818
He never, he never said no.
他从来没有，他从来没有说过不。

00:19:32.818 --> 00:19:35.860
Really in the end, I can't blame anybody but myself.
说到底，我真的只能怪我自己。

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:41.711
I think that I just, I a little bit chickened out in terms of like really pushing the content there.
我觉得我只是……在真正要把内容推向那个方向时，我稍微有点怂了。

00:19:41.711 --> 00:19:44.109
It was still fresh enough and new enough.
当时一切还足够新鲜。

00:19:44.109 --> 00:19:46.061
The show wasn't really about that.
这部剧本来也不是讲这个的。

00:19:46.061 --> 00:19:51.055
But what was weird is that we were writing these characters that I felt the closest to.
但诡异的是，我们在创作这些我感觉最亲近的角色。

00:19:51.055 --> 00:19:54.269
Marshall and Lily were the most like myself and my wife.
马歇尔和莉莉最像我和我的妻子。

00:19:54.269 --> 00:20:11.990
when they had a healthy baby on the show, which again, we wrote, I felt somewhat estranged from them the same way that I felt estranged from you, Alec, from my other friends that at the same time as me and your son was born days apart from mine, we're gonna get very real here and break dragging you into the realness.
当他们在剧中生下一个健康的宝宝时——这再次说明，这是我们写出来的——我感到自己和他们有些疏离，就如同我感到自己和你疏离一样，亚历克，从我其他的那些朋友身上——在那个时期，你的儿子和我儿子出生的日子只差几天，我们要来点超现实的真实对话了，把你拉进这种真实中来。

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:14.069
I'm not gonna be the only one in the fucking hot seat here, bro.
我可不能成为唯一一个坐在该死的主角座位上面临烤问的人，老兄。

00:20:14.069 --> 00:20:17.990
No, don't worry, it's in my list of questions.
不，别担心，这在我的问题清单上呢。

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:19.309
We'll get there.
我们待会儿就聊到这。

00:20:19.309 --> 00:20:24.470
And I'm not at all singling, Like everyone started to have kids.
我绝对不是在针对谁，就像每个人都开始生孩子一样。

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:31.150
We were only having kids on the early side and everyone's kid seemed so shiny and perfect and easy.
我们只是比较早生孩子的，而且每个人的孩子看起来都那么光鲜、完美、好带。

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:40.549
And everything was going according to plan and you feel, and I hope there are other parents like myself watching, it feels so lonely at first.
一切都按计划进行，而你会觉得——我希望有其他像我一样的父母在看节目——一开始真的感到非常孤独。

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:46.349
And especially if there's anybody younger than me with young kids still, I hope you know, I see you.
特别是如果还有任何比我年轻、家里依然有幼童的人，我希望你知道，我看到你们了。

00:20:46.349 --> 00:20:47.710
It's so lonesome.
那种感觉太孤单了。

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:55.009
And then when everyone around you who seems to not be having your incredibly lonely, challenging experience, it just compounds that loneliness.
然后，当看到你周围的每个人似乎都没有经历你那种无比孤独、充满挑战的遭遇时，这种对比只会加剧你的孤独感。

00:20:55.009 --> 00:21:02.470
And suddenly I felt that way a little bit towards Marshall and Lily, like, ah, look, they went and had a perfectly healthy, easy kid too.
突然间，我对马歇尔和莉莉也有了那么一点这种感觉，就像是，啊，瞧啊，他们也去生了一个完美健康、轻轻松松的孩子。

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:05.309
But they're fictional, and one of the people writing that.
但他们是虚构的，而我就是创作他们的人之一。

00:21:05.309 --> 00:21:06.509
It was just really bizarre.
这真的太怪异了。

00:21:06.509 --> 00:21:07.349
You did that.
这是你干的。

00:21:07.349 --> 00:21:08.190
I did that, right.
是我干的，没错。

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:10.349
I could have, maybe we could have done something different, but that's what I did.
我本可以……也许我们本可以做点不同的选择，但这就是我做的事。

00:21:10.349 --> 00:21:13.230
And I'm like, fuck them with their healthy, perfect kid.
当时我就想，去他们的健康完美小孩吧。

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:17.269
But I got over that, and I just embraced writing Marshall and Lily, our fictional characters.
但我挺过来了，我也开始欣然接受去创作马歇尔和莉莉，我们虚构的角色。

00:21:17.269 --> 00:21:19.029
They had to be fictional characters.
他们必须是虚构的角色。

00:21:19.029 --> 00:21:21.349
it was nice to have a distance from them at times.
有时和他们保持一些距离其实挺好的。

00:21:21.349 --> 00:21:26.089
And I think probably my deeper need and why I was the one that didn't fully push.
我想，可能我内心深处更需要的，也是为什么我没有完全去争取的原因。

00:21:26.089 --> 00:21:34.809
I didn't like die on this hill to make them have a complicated kid so I could write about that probably in part because I wanted to have somewhere else to go to.
我并没有为了让他们生一个复杂的孩子而死守这个阵地，这样我就可以把那段经历写下来，可能部分原因是因为我想去别的地方。

00:21:34.809 --> 00:21:43.049
You know, I wanted to have my home life be that problem solving and then go to work and have something else.
你知道的，我想让我的家庭生活去解决那些问题，然后去工作，做点别的。

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:57.544
But it always stuck with me that I always wanted to write something this huge life-changing part of my life and I tried for years to figure that out and I'd say this book is the culmination.
但这件事一直萦绕在我心头，我一直想把我人生中这个巨大而改变命运的部分写下来，我花了多年时间去琢磨，我觉得这本书就是它的结晶。

00:21:57.544 --> 00:21:59.963
I finally got there with this book.
我终于通过这本书做到了。

00:21:59.963 --> 00:22:27.338
So let's talk a little bit about the interim between how I met your mother in writing this book which Hollywood was very good to you for quite a while and you were very good to it and then I know you had some less great experiences confronting executives, confronting people who maybe didn't, weren't as into the idea of this sort of representation on screen, this sort of storytelling on screen.
那么我们来聊聊从《老爸老妈浪漫史》结束到创作这本书之间的那段过渡期，好莱坞对你很不错，持续了相当长一段时间，你对它也很不错，然后我听说你有一些不太好的经历，面对高管们，面对那些可能并不太看好这种银幕代表性、这种银幕叙事方式的人。

00:22:27.338 --> 00:22:33.905
I imagine some of that anger that that must have made you feel has made it into into this book.
我猜，那种愤怒感——你当时一定感受到的愤怒——也已经融入了这本书中。

00:22:33.905 --> 00:22:45.450
Can you talk, I don't know what sort of detail you want to go into with that, but can you talk a little bit about trying to make other works of art that did very much.
你能聊聊吗？我不知道你想谈到什么程度的细节，但你能聊聊尝试创作其他艺术作品的经历吗？那些作品确实……

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:49.130
Yeah, I'll tell a little anecdote just to capture how it felt.
好的，我讲个小插曲，来捕捉一下当时的感觉。

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:55.900
And I wrote about this, I wrote this little, this S personal essay in the Boston Globe called Hollywood in the R word, if anyone wants to look that up.
我写过这个，我写了一篇短文，一篇发表在《波士顿环球报》上的个人随笔，题目叫《好莱坞与R开头的词》，如果有人想去查阅的话。

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:57.289
It's, I was really proud of it.
我为它感到非常自豪。

00:22:57.289 --> 00:23:02.009
It was one of the steps towards me writing this novel, was writing that piece.
写那篇文章，是我创作这部小说迈出的步骤之一。

00:23:02.569 --> 00:23:18.325
And in it, I briefly touched upon this one story, which was a couple of years after How much a mother ended we Carter and I signed a deal to develop TV shows for another place And I will throw no one under the bus and no one's evil in the story And I will not name names.
在文章中，我简短地提到了这样一个故事：在《老爸老妈浪漫史》结束几年后，我和卡特签了一份协议，为另一个地方开发电视剧。我不会把任何人推出去当替罪羊，这个故事里也没有恶人，我也不会点名。

00:23:18.325 --> 00:23:19.139
It's not back.
不是这样的。

00:23:19.139 --> 00:23:46.929
That's not what I'm doing here but I We signed this deal and I thought the folks were sending the deal with new about Elliot knew about my son and knew about the journey I've been through we'd thrown these big like fundraiser parties on the how I met your mother soundstage and we'd had like we'd done events and And I came into this deal at this new place thinking this is when I'll write something about my version of parenthood, being the parent of a child with a disability.
我来这里不是为了这个，但我们签了这份协议，我以为签协议的人了解埃利奥特，了解我的儿子，也了解我经历过的心路历程。我们曾在《老爸老妈浪漫史》的片场举办过大型的募捐派对，我们办过各种活动，而且我带着这种想法进入了这家新公司的协议：这就是我将要写下属于我的为人父母版本的时候，作为一个残疾儿童的父母。我会在这份新协议下找到一种方法，让其中的某一部剧或两部剧，你知道的，也许这会成为我们推介的一堆项目中的一个元素，这是一个为期三年的协议。我会有这个机会。

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:57.250
I will find a way under this new deal where some one of the shows or two of the shows, you know, maybe this will be an element and a bunch of things we pitched was going to be a three year deal.
我会有这个机会。

00:23:57.250 --> 00:23:58.769
I'll have the opportunity.
我会有这个机会。

00:23:58.769 --> 00:24:15.980
And in the opening meeting of the deal, some of the executives in the room started telling a story that the gist of which was mocking some adults with disabilities that would eat lunch at a park near where they all worked every day.
而在协议的首次会议上，房间里的几位高管开始讲一个故事，大意是嘲笑一些成年残疾人，他们每天都会在他们工作地点附近的公园里吃午饭。

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:26.940
They would all sometimes go eat lunch on the park and they were joking about how they were uncomfortable being near the table full of adults who lived in a group home nearby in the area who would go eat lunch there.
他们有时都会去公园吃午饭，然后开玩笑说，他们对于坐在附近一张坐满了成年人的桌子旁感到很不自在，那些成年人住在附近的一个集体宿舍里，经常去那里吃午饭。

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:31.259
The gist of the story was them joking about like, aren't we terrible for feeling upset?
那个故事的大意是他们在开玩笑，比如：我们因为感到心烦意乱是不是太糟糕了？

00:24:31.259 --> 00:24:47.069
So they kind of were saying it sucks that I'm worth saying this, but it was still had this feeling of like, we're still kind of making the joke about those those folks are like, yeah, anyway, we don't really go over there so much anymore to that park and have lunch there.
所以他们有点像是在说，我说出这话感觉真糟，但它仍然有一种感觉，就是我们依然在拿那些人开玩笑，就像是“对，不管怎样，我们现在不怎么去那个公园吃午饭了”。

00:24:47.069 --> 00:24:49.190
And it was a bummer.
这真让人沮丧。

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:53.670
And I sat there frozen in silence and I didn't say anything.
我坐在那里，僵硬地沉默着，什么也没说。

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:57.390
This was the first meeting on the first day of a three year deal.
这是为期三年的协议的第一天的第一次会议。

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:02.819
And it was very loudly in many Hollywood papers.
而且这在许多好莱坞报纸上都被大肆报道过。

00:25:02.819 --> 00:25:06.859
A three year deal on which I thought we would write about this.
一份为期三年的协议，我本以为我们会以此为题材进行创作。

00:25:06.859 --> 00:25:09.619
We would tell stories about this with our new partners.
我们会与新伙伴一起讲述这方面的故事。

00:25:09.619 --> 00:25:12.339
And now here's the part that redeems these folks.
现在，这就是能让这些人得到救赎的部分。

00:25:12.339 --> 00:25:13.660
And again, no one was evil.
再说一次，没有人是恶人。

00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:17.500
This is an example of no one knows how to talk about this shit.
这就是一个没人知道该如何谈论这破事的例子。

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:25.819
No one, people are uncomfortable with disability because we have all grown up in a society where we don't know how to talk about disability.
没有人，人们对残疾感到不自在，因为我们都在一个不知道如何谈论残疾的社会中长大。

00:25:25.819 --> 00:25:27.579
And I don't blame them for it.
我不怪他们。

00:25:27.579 --> 00:25:30.440
I was the same way before I had my son.
在我有儿子之前，我也一样。

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:31.960
I'm no better or different.
我并没有比别人更好或更不同。

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:37.000
And I like walked out of that meeting, absolutely numb.
我走出那场会议时，整个人完全麻木了。

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:38.039
Carter looks over at me.
卡特看着我。

00:25:38.039 --> 00:25:40.680
He's like, are you about to vomit?
他说：“你是不是要吐了？”

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.599
You know what's going on?
“你知道怎么回事吗？”

00:25:42.599 --> 00:25:44.200
He knew how bad it was.
他知道事情有多糟糕。

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.319
And he was wondering if I would say something and I didn't.
他想知道我会不会说点什么，但我没有。

00:25:46.319 --> 00:25:50.000
And then we're like driving away and I go, I have to say something.
然后我们开车离开，我说：“我必须说点什么。”

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:53.119
And we get to our back to our office, wherever we were.
我们回到办公室，无论我们在哪里。

00:25:53.119 --> 00:26:00.480
And I call them up and I say, Hey, listen, I went completely catatonically into a fugue state during that meeting.
我打电话给他们，我说：“嘿，听着，我在那次会议上完全陷入了魂不守舍的僵死状态。”

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:06.960
I got these folks, these executives on, and I said, here's what that was for me, and I told them, and they felt terrible about it.
我找到了这些人，这些高管，我对他们说：“对我来说，刚才那是怎么回事”，我把感受告诉了他们，他们对此感到非常糟糕。

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:13.039
They gave some context about why they were feeling some of their discomfort that had to do with some of their own personal lives, too.
他们也讲了一些背景，解释了他们为什么会感到不自在，这其实也跟他们自己的一些个人生活有关。

00:26:13.039 --> 00:26:22.359
And as always happens when you give people a chance that they dimensionalize themselves in telling that story, and we stayed in that deal and we worked with those people and we had a good time.
就像你给别人一个机会时总是会发生的那样，他们在讲那个故事时展现了他们的立体感，我们留在那份协议里，和那些人一起工作，度过了一段美好的时光。

00:26:22.359 --> 00:26:25.960
It was, this is not their evil we quit that day.
这并不是说他们是恶人，我们当天就辞职了。

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:32.559
But I was so happy that I had said that I actually, it was so ashamed in the moment that I didn't say something, but then happy that I finally did.
但我很高兴我说了出来，其实当时没说出口让我感到非常羞愧，但后来我终于说出来了，这让我很高兴。

00:26:32.559 --> 00:26:42.380
This is all to say, that's what I felt up against in trying to pitch stories about this, even in the 2000s where this story takes place.
这一切都是为了说明，这就是我在尝试推销这类故事时所感受到的阻力，即使在故事发生的2000年代也是如此。

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:45.000
And long story short, we tried.
长话短说，我们试过了。

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:49.759
And this is not me blaming the people we were allied with in trying to pitch these shows.
我并不是在责怪我们当时尝试推销这些剧集时结盟的伙伴。

00:26:49.759 --> 00:26:50.599
No one bought them.
没有人买单。

00:26:50.599 --> 00:26:54.599
We pitched a few different ideas.
我们推销了几个不同的创意。

00:26:54.599 --> 00:26:59.839
We wrote a feature film, nothing ever that set within this world of disability.
我们写过一部故事片，但从来没有以这个残障世界为背景。

00:26:59.839 --> 00:27:06.119
And we tried a few different ways that at comedy, a musical, we tried a whole bunch, nothing ever found its way through.
我们尝试过几种不同的方向：喜剧、音乐剧，试了各种各样，但没有一个能顺利通过。

00:27:06.119 --> 00:27:24.240
And I think that's not an uncommon story where studios will want the nobility of giving it the old college tribe and in the end when it comes to putting millions of dollars into a TV show that might go for years, It's often not what gets chosen to spend those millions of dollars on.
我想这并不是一个罕见的现象：制片厂或许会有那种“拼尽全力、展现高尚”的冲动，但到头来，当涉及到要把数百万美元投入一部可能会播好几年的电视剧时，这往往不会成为他们决定砸下数百万资金的首选。

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:29.640
And that now I'll race to the end of the story, which is that's why I wrote it as a book.
现在我快进到结局，那就是为什么我把它写成了一本书。

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:33.160
I wanted, I needed to tell a story about this as a writer.
作为一个作家，我想，我也需要讲述一个关于这个主题的故事。

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:42.640
And the book includes some of what I just talked about, but the book is not just about Hollywood, the book is about a family.
书里包含了刚刚我提到的一些内容，但这本书不仅关于好莱坞，它还关于一个家庭。

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:48.730
And this old man, he musted me, fell in love with you.
而这个老头，他一定……我爱上你了。

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:52.470
New York City.
纽约市。

00:27:52.470 --> 00:28:01.930
And now, commercials.
现在是广告时间。

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:05.630
End of commercials, back to show.
广告结束，回到节目。

