Elliot Thomas tells us, with the Golden Globe award, for best novel goes to Craig Thomas.
埃利奥特·托马斯告诉我们，金球奖最佳小说奖得主是克雷格·托马斯。

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:19.950
That's good, I never won shit on how I met your mother.
太棒了，我在《老爸老妈浪漫史》里可从来没拿过什么破奖。

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:22.390
I'm already winning Golden Globes for my book.
我的书竟然已经赢得金球奖了。

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:27.109
I didn't know they, I wasn't unaware they added a book category, thanks, else for telling me that.
我不知道，我以前不知道他们增加了一个图书奖项，谢谢埃尔斯告诉我这个。

00:28:27.109 --> 00:28:36.230
Well, let's stick with the personal for one more second, then we'll broaden out how you wrote this book and a bunch of these great questions that people are asking.
好吧，我们再把话题留在个人层面上多一会儿，然后我们再把范围扩大，聊聊你是如何写这本书的，以及大家提出的这些精彩问题。

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:39.309
So yes, I was going to say it too, but I'll say more.
对，我本来也想这么说，不过我会说得更详细点。

00:28:39.309 --> 00:28:46.430
Your son and mine were born, I think, one day apart, if my math is right.
如果我没记错的话，你的儿子和我的儿子出生只差了一天。

00:28:46.430 --> 00:29:22.819
And people who know about us a little bit, I went to high school with your wife, I knew her before you did you and I became friends very close to day one of Wesleyan University where your your characters go to school went to school and we were all of a sudden about to share this and I'm bringing this up because not just so that everyone could hear our personal story you and me but I'm people clearly have to go through this both on your side and on my side of what was this this ledger, and we were gonna share everything, right?
稍微了解我们一点的人都知道，我和你妻子是高中同学，在你认识她之前我就认识她了；你和我则在卫斯理大学开学的第一天就成了非常要好的朋友——也就是你笔下那些角色上过学的大学。然后我们突然就要分享这一切了，我提起这个不仅是为了让大家听到你和我的个人故事，更是因为显然每个人都必须经历这一关，无论是在你的立场还是我的立场上，我们都曾在这本账簿上面对这一切，我们本该分享一切，对吧？

00:29:22.819 --> 00:29:29.019
Our wives, our friends, they were gonna share that and we were gonna share this, and it was like drop of a hat.
我们的妻子，我们的朋友，他们会分享那些，而我们也会分享这些，但结果却说变就变。

00:29:29.019 --> 00:29:32.059
Nope, we're not gonna share anything.
不，我们什么都不会分享。

00:29:32.059 --> 00:29:37.779
It made it sound briefly like we were sharing wives that was not exactly what's gonna happen, but let's move on back to the deep part.
刚才听起来好像我们要共妻似的，这可不是接下来要发生的事，不过我们还是言归正传，回到深刻的部分吧。

00:29:37.779 --> 00:29:39.299
We'll do some editing on that.
这段我们会剪辑一下。

00:29:40.299 --> 00:29:41.700
It's live, but okay, keep going.
这是直播，不过好吧，继续。

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:48.420
Now on the podcast, you guys, you've been making some amends for some of the lines that don't age very well.
现在在这个播客里，你们一直在为一些经不起时间考验的台词进行弥补和道歉。

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:58.420
And that's very important, and we wanna really thank, by the way, everyone who has commented on how much you've appreciated that too, because it's very important.
这非常重要，顺便说一句，我们要由衷地感谢所有评论表示对此大加赞赏的人，因为这真的很重要。

00:30:01.259 --> 00:30:16.700
On page 67, page, the mother here says, there's a phrase parents of healthy neurotypical kids like to say to special needs, parents quote unquote, after we've told them about something difficult with our child.
在第67页，这里写道，健康的神经典型儿童的父母喜欢对特种需求儿童的父母说这样一句话，当我们向他们倾诉了关于我们孩子的某种困境之后。

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:19.460
They think this makes us feel better, less alone.
他们以为这会让我们感觉好一点，不那么孤单。

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:23.099
And that phrase is, hey, that's just how it is with every kid, right?
那句话就是：“嘿，哪个孩子不都是这样嘛，对吧？”

00:30:23.099 --> 00:30:25.900
Meaning we all worry, so we're all the same.
意思是大家都会担心，所以我们都一样。

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:27.859
I'm sorry, but no, it's not the same.
对不起，但并不，这根本不一样。

00:30:27.859 --> 00:30:33.019
Your kid is very, very different from mine, and we need to be able to talk about that.
你的孩子和我的孩子有着天壤之别，我们需要能够谈论这一点。

00:30:33.019 --> 00:30:37.019
Let me just say to you here publicly, and then I will bring it home.
在此，我先公开对你说一声，然后我会把这个话题引向深处。

00:30:37.019 --> 00:30:42.180
I hope I never said anything quite like that, but there is a distinct non-zero chance that I did.
我希望自己从来没说过类似的话，但我的确有极大的可能性曾经说过。

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:45.759
And if I did, I want to apologize here.
如果我真说过，我想在这里道歉。

00:30:45.759 --> 00:30:51.920
we didn't wind up sharing our early fatherhoods together and I'm sad about that.
我们最终没有一起度过为人父母的早期阶段，对此我感到很遗憾。

00:30:51.920 --> 00:31:01.759
And the reason I say that that's on me is because like you're saying, I don't blame myself for not knowing what to say.
我说这是我自己的问题，原因就像你说的，我并不怪自己当时不知道该说些什么。

00:31:01.759 --> 00:31:08.440
I mean, I think I sort of pretended I didn't have a son for a long time around you sort of, you know?
我的意思是，我觉得在很长一段时间里，在你面前我好像假装自己没有儿子一样，你知道的？

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:09.799
You're not the only one.
你不是唯一一个。

00:31:09.799 --> 00:31:10.299
Right.
没错。

00:31:10.299 --> 00:31:12.990
And I think that we're the blame because that that's normal.
我觉得问题出在我们身上，因为那很正常。

00:31:12.759 --> 00:31:22.960
The blame comes from, I wish I had done a Lillian Marshall-esque timeout and go, okay, got, Craig, I know we got some shit going on here.
错就错在，我希望当时能来一个莉莲·马歇尔风格的暂停，然后说：“好吧，听着，克雷格，我知道我们这里现在有些乱七八糟的事。”

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:24.640
Let's you and I take a pause for a second.
“你和我暂停一下。”

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:29.640
Walk over here and talk about how we can talk about this.
“走到这边来聊聊我们该如何讨论这件事。”

00:31:30.440 --> 00:31:45.200
Talk about how we could live together like this because we kind of wound up not living together for quite a long time and I should have asked more and I'm very grateful that we're now making this podcast and we're very much in each other's lives right now.
“聊聊我们该如何这样共同生活，因为我们俩差不多有很长一段时间没能真正生活在一起了，我本该多问一些的。我很感激我们现在能一起做这个播客，而且我们现在的生活也紧密交织在一起。”

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.359
But, so I apologize for that.
所以，为此我感到抱歉。

00:31:47.359 --> 00:31:49.680
Yeah, oh no, but it's tough.
噢，不，不过这确实很难。

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:53.680
I appreciate that it's both ways too, because I didn't know how to talk about it either.
我很感激这也是双向的，因为我当时也不知道该怎么谈论它。

00:31:53.680 --> 00:32:16.440
I didn't know how to, I didn't know how important it would be to get over the feeling of being disincluded because we had this different child and how it would have been worth making that leap back to my friends who were having kids and that feeling of, well, it's too hard to see the other version that's going so much easier than we're having it.
我不知道如何去克服那种被排斥感——因为我们有一个与众不同的孩子；我也不知道克服这种感觉有多重要，从而去重新向我那些正在养育孩子的朋友们靠拢。那种感觉就像是：去看那些比我们轻松得多的育儿版本实在太难了。

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:26.160
So I'm going to not see that and being on alert for anything that's going to be said or done that's not going to be exactly right so that I can go ask, see, they don't get it.
所以我决定不去看，并且时刻警惕着任何会被说出或做出的、哪怕有一点点不对劲的言行，好让我能去质问：“看吧，他们根本不懂。”

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:29.480
And I'm not saying just you, but everybody.
我指的不仅是你，而是所有人。

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:33.279
And if I could go back, now we'll be real hymium-speaky.
如果我能回到过去——现在我们要玩点怀旧时空穿梭的把戏了。

00:32:33.279 --> 00:32:39.440
And if I could like flash back and revisit my past self, I would say stop that.
如果我能像闪回一样重新审视过去的自己，我会对自己说：住手。

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:50.640
I would say, find a way to stay connected to your friends, even as they have kids who feel different from your kid, and there's some jealousy, and there's some look how easy they have it.
我会说：想办法和你的朋友保持联系，哪怕他们的孩子感觉和你的孩子不同，哪怕这其中有些许嫉妒，有些许“看他们带孩子多轻松”的感慨。

00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:59.589
Sitting in that world of, I'm gonna put a distance here because I am uncomfortable with this or they don't get it.
陷在那种世界里——“我要在这里划清界限，因为我对这感到不舒服”或者“他们根本不懂”。

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:06.069
It's just, it is a recipe to live in a stupid pain cave that you don't have to live in.
这简直就是，这就是自寻烦恼、作茧自缚的秘诀，而你本不必过这种日子的。

00:33:06.069 --> 00:33:07.150
And this is the problem.
这就是问题所在。

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:14.829
It's the same thing as I said about those executive folks, Like, none of us grow up knowing how to talk about this stuff and it seems other.
这跟我刚才说那些高管的话是一样的，我们谁都不是生来就知道该怎么谈论这些事的，而且它显得格格不入。

00:33:14.829 --> 00:33:28.309
It seems like something that will happen to someone else, disability is something that will happen to someone else, then it comes into your life and you realize you've been ill-equipped to even know how to talk about it or approach it and most people you know, it's the same thing.
残障这件事看起来就像是会发生在别人身上的事，会降临在别人身上，直到它闯入你的生活，你才意识到自己根本毫无准备，甚至不知道该如何谈论它或接近它。你认识的大多数人也是如此，情况完全一样。

00:33:28.309 --> 00:33:45.109
You're not conversant in this language and that's the problem when we don't include people with disabilities in society and tell stories about them and connect to them and have them be visible and really part of society and have roles and have a place and inclusion and inclusion.
你对这种语言一无所知，而这正是当我们在社会中不包容残障人士、不讲述他们的故事、不与他们建立联系、不让他们被看见并真正成为社会的一部分、不让他们拥有角色和地位、不实现包容时所带来的问题。

00:33:45.109 --> 00:33:46.626
It's a good word that word.
包容，这个词真好。

00:33:46.626 --> 00:33:47.749
It's not a bad word.
这不是个贬义词。

00:33:47.749 --> 00:33:50.725
Don't trust any fucking person that's that different.
不要相信任何认为如此大相径庭的混蛋。

00:33:50.725 --> 00:33:53.533
And that is how we all learn how to talk about it.
这就是我们大家学习如何谈论它的方式。

00:33:53.533 --> 00:33:56.902
But we came of age at a time where we were not getting that.
但我们成长的时代并没有提供这些。

00:33:56.902 --> 00:33:58.699
We were growing up in the 1980s.
我们在20世纪80年代长大。

00:33:58.699 --> 00:34:02.349
And there we were as new parents and we didn't have the language.
然后我们成了新手父母，却缺乏这种语言。

00:34:02.349 --> 00:34:19.590
And I think it's the same thing as that story about the executive of folks like we just, there's a discomfort, there's a discomfort and there's a we don't know what to say so we're not gonna say anything or you try to say something and it's kind of wrong and then did I offend that person, blah, blah, blah.
我觉得这和那个关于高管的故事如出一辙，我们就是……有一种不适感，有一种不知该说什因此索性什么都不说的不适感，或者你试着说点什么却说得不太对劲，然后开始担心“我是不是冒犯了那个人”诸如此类。

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:21.230
And then you just don't talk about it.
然后你干脆就不谈了。

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:24.590
Then a year goes by and you don't talk about it or two years or three years.
接着一年过去了，你没有谈论它，两年、三年也过去了。

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:27.269
And yeah, I regret that too.
是的，我也为此感到后悔。

00:34:27.269 --> 00:34:30.750
I feel like that happened with me and a bunch of people.
我觉得我和许多人之间都发生过这种情况。

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:44.809
And yeah, if I could go back I would change that And I would wish, I'm happy to be talking about this because I hope people might hear it and question whether they're doing that.
是的，如果能回到过去，我会改变这一切，我希望能谈论这些，因为我希望人们听到后能反思自己是否也在这样做。

00:34:44.809 --> 00:34:52.929
If they're parents of young kids with new diagnoses, try to stay connected and also it's really hard.
如果他们是刚拿到孩子诊断证明的幼童父母，请试着保持联系，尽管这真的很难。

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:59.090
It's really hard to see other people seeming to breeze through parenthood.
看着别人似乎轻而易举地度过育儿期，真的很难。

00:34:59.090 --> 00:35:02.329
And then they're still complaining about it and you're going, what the fuck are you complaining about?
然后他们还在抱怨，而你在想：“你们他妈的在抱怨什么？”

00:35:02.329 --> 00:35:03.369
You have it so easy.
你们过得可太轻松了。

00:35:03.369 --> 00:35:04.409
Oh, you didn't sleep out.
哦，你们没睡好觉？

00:35:04.409 --> 00:35:06.250
Wow, my son has just cut open.
哇，我儿子的胸膛刚被剖开过呢。

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:07.070
Fuck off.
滚蛋吧。

00:35:08.889 --> 00:35:13.650
And it's, and in the end, no one wins when you do that, but of course you do that.
而且，到头来，当你这样做时没有人是赢家，但你情不自禁地还是会这样做。

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:17.289
And you find yourself watching years go by and you're doing that.
然后你发现几年时间就这样白白流逝，而你一直在这么做。

00:35:17.289 --> 00:35:22.289
And then thank God one day you get fucking bored of being angry and judgy.
谢天谢地，有一天你终于对那种愤怒和评判的态度感到厌烦了。

00:35:22.289 --> 00:35:24.090
And you just go, I'm just lonely now.
然后你只会说：“我现在只是感到孤独。”

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:25.730
I just want to connect with my friends.
“我只是想和我的朋友们建立联系。”

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:33.039
And if anybody's listening and you can speed through that process faster than I did, I would recommend that.
如果有人正在听节目，并且能比我更快地走完这个心路历程，那我绝对会推荐这样做。

00:35:33.039 --> 00:35:48.639
Well, I think that's something that I know that you're, I wanna talk about how much you think your book has already started to do this, or I know you're hoped for it to do this, but the idea of creating community is something that I think you and Carter definitely didn't set out to do with how I met your mother.
嗯，我觉得这也是我想说的，我知道你……我想聊聊你觉得你的书在这方面已经起到了多少作用，或者我知道你对它寄予的期望，但创造社群这个概念，我觉得你和卡特在创作《老爸老妈浪漫史》时绝对不是一开始就刻意去做的。

00:35:48.639 --> 00:35:50.559
I mean, you're just gonna write your TV show.
我是说，你们当时只是在写你们的电视剧。

00:35:50.559 --> 00:36:07.119
And what we've seen, you saw it all these decades, but we're really seeing what this podcast is, you created a global community, right, with how I met your mother, And we are hoping to hear from them and encourage that.
而我们所看到的——你在过去的几十年里都见证过，但通过这个播客我们真正看到的是——你通过《老爸老妈浪漫史》创造了一个全球性的社群，对吧？我们希望能听到他们的声音并鼓励这种交流。

00:36:07.119 --> 00:36:13.599
And I think that this too, I know you were on Jamie Lee Seagler and Christina Abigayt's podcast also.
我觉得这也是，我知道你之前也上过杰米·李·柯蒂斯（注：此处应为杰米·李·西格勒 Jamie-Lynn Sigler）和克里斯蒂娜·艾伯盖特的播客。

00:36:13.599 --> 00:36:18.360
And they were talking about just the loneliness of going through so much.
他们当时在谈论经历这一切时的孤独感。

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:24.159
Of going through when you get some sort of diagnosis and your life starts to change or your child does.
当你收到某种诊断结果，你的生活或你的孩子的生活开始发生改变时的那种经历。

00:36:24.159 --> 00:36:33.000
And this idea that you are again now creating hopefully or becoming part of not creating necessarily but becoming a voice in this community.
而这种你现在再次——希望能够——创造的理念，或者说成为这个社群的一部分（不一定是从无到有地创造，而是成为其中的一个声音）。

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:33.840
I think it's really helpful.
我觉得这真的很有帮助。

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:41.030
I'll tell you right now, we have M-Beth G78s, as thank you, Craig, I felt seen.
我现在就告诉你，我们有M-Beth G78留言说：“谢谢你，克雷格，我感到了被看见。”

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:44.869
Katie K-N-C says...
Katie K-N-C说……

00:36:44.869 --> 00:36:45.789
Thank you, Emily.
谢谢你，艾米丽。

00:36:45.789 --> 00:36:47.510
Emily and I are buddies online.
我和艾米丽是网上的好朋友。

00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:48.349
Thank you, Emily.
谢谢你，艾米丽。

00:36:48.349 --> 00:36:49.190
I really appreciate.
我真的很感激。

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:56.030
Emily made a beautiful video about reading my book and I know Emily is just a great disability advocate herself.
艾米丽做了一个非常漂亮的视频，关于阅读我的书，我知道艾米丽本人就是一位杰出的残障倡导者。

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:56.869
She has disability.
她有残障。

00:36:56.869 --> 00:36:59.630
I know she has a completely different type of deletion, I believe in Elliot.
我知道她患有一种完全不同的基因缺失，我相信是在埃利奥特。

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:14.850
I'm getting that randomly, but you're so eloquent about it and you you it's gonna say you inspire me But we've actually made jokes about how like don't force me to be inspiring Which is in my book and and she talks about that and like anyway, it's just very meaningful.
我随口这么一说，但你对此表达得非常流利，你本来要说“你激励了我”，但我们实际上还开过玩笑说，比如“别逼我去当什么励志模范”（这也是我在书里写过的），她也谈到了这一点，总之，这真的很有意义。

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:16.661
So thank you Emily That's great.
所以谢谢你，艾米丽，这太棒了。

00:37:16.661 --> 00:37:20.282
Now someone I apologize here It says kd.knc and then next to it.
现在，有人……我在这里道个歉，上面写着 kd.knc，紧挨着它。

00:37:20.282 --> 00:37:37.709
It says at Michelle McNeil 009 I'm not Convert I'm 50 so I don't understand Instagram enough to know which is the name of the person who has said this but But they said, I have Mosaic Down Syndrome, meaning one of my cells has the extra chromosome and I found extremely accurate and amazingly representative.
上面写着 @Michelle McNeil 009，我不太擅长这个，我50岁了，所以对 Instagram 还不够了解，不知道哪一个是说这话的人的名字，但……但他们说，我有嵌合型唐氏综合征，也就是说我的细胞中有一个额外的染色体，我发现（这本书）极其准确且具有惊人的代表性。

00:37:37.710 --> 00:37:39.510
Oh, that's so nice to hear.
哦，听到这个太好了。

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:41.389
That is so nice to hear in such a gift.
听到这个真是太棒了，也是一份如此厚重的礼物。

00:37:41.389 --> 00:37:46.230
The disability community has been so amazing in receiving this book.
残障群体在接受这本书时表现得如此热情。

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:50.510
It's written with, I wrote it with love for my son, but I don't have a disability.
它是用……我是带着对我儿子的爱写下这本书的，但我没有残障。

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:54.789
I'm a parent and I'm writing in these four characters, one of whom has a disability.
我是一位家长，我在写这四个角色，其中有一个角色有残障。

00:37:54.789 --> 00:38:05.530
A lot of it's my son, a lot of it's the 18 years I spent with my meeting all these amazing families, all these amazing kids and all these amazing now young adults with disabilities, but of course I was nervous putting it out in the world.
其中很大一部分取材于我的儿子，很大一部分取材于我度过的这18年里，我遇到了所有这些了不起的家庭、所有这些了不起的孩子，以及现在所有这些了不起的、患有残障的年轻人，但当然，把这本书推向世界时我还是很紧张的。

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:06.829
I wanted to get it right.
我想把它写对。

00:38:06.829 --> 00:38:12.670
I wanted people to feel like it was authentic and that it gave voice, especially to the character who has Down Syndrome in the right way.
我希望人们觉得它是真实的，并且它确实发出了声音，尤其是以正确的方式为患有唐氏综合征的角色发声。

00:38:12.670 --> 00:38:14.030
So that means a lot.
所以这意义重大。

00:38:14.030 --> 00:38:15.670
Thank you for sharing that comment.
谢谢你分享这条评论。

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:17.309
And by the way, I've now figured things out.
顺便说一句，我现在弄清楚了。

00:38:17.309 --> 00:38:20.469
That was from kd.knc because I now see what Michelle said.
那是来自 kd.knc 的，因为我现在看到米歇尔说的话了。

00:38:20.469 --> 00:38:27.103
Michelle McNeala 09 asks, have you received any feedback from people with Down syndrome and or their families?
Michelle McNeala 09 问：你收到过任何来自唐氏综合征患者和/或他们家人的反馈吗？

00:38:27.103 --> 00:38:32.169
How do you feel about your portrayal of how they feel about your portrayal of Emmet?
你觉得他们对你塑造埃姆特（Emmet）的看法有何感受？

00:38:32.169 --> 00:38:36.150
Have you received any criticism or do people feel it was accurate?
你收到过任何批评，还是人们觉得它很准确？

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:43.053
I've been overwhelmed by how joyful and warm the reception has been to the character of Emmet specifically.
人们对埃姆特这个角色的接受度如此欢快和热烈，这让我感到受宠若惊。

00:38:43.053 --> 00:38:45.311
It's been beyond my wildest dreams.
这超出了我最疯狂的想象。

00:38:45.311 --> 00:38:54.343
It's just been so warmly received and people take time to reach out from all over the world and write to me in various ways to tell me that.
它的反响实在是太热烈了，来自世界各地的人们都抽出时间通过各种方式联系我、写信告诉我这些。

00:38:54.343 --> 00:38:59.439
It does feel like Emmett is really the heart and soul in the star of this book.
感觉埃姆特确实是这本书的心脏、灵魂和明星。

00:39:00.719 --> 00:39:03.948
I wanted to get the Down Syndrome part as right as I could.
我想尽可能把唐氏综合征的部分写得准确。

00:39:03.948 --> 00:39:05.808
My son doesn't have Down Syndrome.
我儿子没有唐氏综合征。

00:39:05.808 --> 00:39:07.504
My son has something different.
我儿子的情况有所不同。

00:39:07.504 --> 00:39:13.687
There are a lot of overlaps to Down Syndrome with Jacob Sin syndrome, which my son has, but it is also different.
雅各布辛综合征（我儿子患有的病症）与唐氏综合征有很多重叠之处，但它也有所不同。

00:39:13.687 --> 00:39:19.651
I had a good friend, one of the founders of the inclusion school, my son went to from kindergarten to age 18.
我曾有一个好朋友，是我儿子从幼儿园到18岁所就读的融合学校的创始人之一。

00:39:19.651 --> 00:39:35.683
That school was founded by three mothers who had kids with Down syndrome and did not see a just right school that was that would really serve their kids atypical learning style, all of their strengths being grown and served and supported and all of their challenges being met in the right way.
那所学校是由三位母亲创立的，她们的孩子患有唐氏综合征，却找不到一所真正适合他们孩子的非典型学习风格、能让孩子的所有长处得到培养和支持、并且所有挑战都能得到正确应对的学校。

00:39:35.683 --> 00:39:46.079
And they like Paige in this book, this was part of the inspiration for Paige, these three moms 15, 20 years ago said, There's no great school here in New York City that's right for our kids.
就像书中的佩奇（Paige）一样，这也是佩奇的灵感来源的一部分，15、20年前，这三位妈妈说：“纽约市没有适合我们孩子的真正好的学校。”

00:39:46.079 --> 00:39:56.483
Fuck it, we're going to make our own and that's the ideal school of Manhattan and one of those mother's, um, Audra, she is the, the parent of this amazingly charismatic, wonderful drummer named Max.
“去他的，我们要自己办一所。”这就是曼哈顿理想学校（The Ideal School of Manhattan），其中一位母亲，嗯，奥德拉（Audra），她是一位非常有个人魅力、极棒的鼓手马克斯（Max）的家长。

00:39:56.483 --> 00:40:02.736
