马克斯比埃利奥特大几岁，所以当埃利奥特长大并在理想学校读书时，我们一直把马克斯当作榜样。

00:40:02.736 --> 00:40:07.413
And I remember watching Max play drums and just kick ass at school events in this school.
我记得看着马克斯打鼓，在学校的活动中表现得极为出色。

00:40:07.413 --> 00:40:12.720
And I'm a drummer and I always, I thought I want to teach my son drums, but will Will it be possible?
而我是一个鼓手，我一直在想，我想教我儿子打鼓，但这可能吗？

00:40:12.719 --> 00:40:14.239
My son has fine motor delays.
我儿子有精细运动发育迟缓。

00:40:14.239 --> 00:40:15.360
He has gross motor delays.
他有粗大运动发育迟缓。

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:18.320
There's all these reasons why it shouldn't have worked.
有这么多理由说明这本行不通。

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:22.239
But here was Max, who has Down Syndrome, just killing it on the drums.
但这里有马克斯，他患有唐氏综合征，却在鼓上大显身手。

00:40:22.239 --> 00:40:25.280
And I said, we have a new role model.
于是我说，我们有了一个新榜样。

00:40:25.280 --> 00:40:28.039
It's Max, and we're gonna get there.
那就是马克斯，我们一定能做到。

00:40:28.039 --> 00:40:34.400
And then during the pandemic, I really locked it down, as it were, and taught my son the drums.
然后在疫情期间，我真正地“闭关修炼”了，可以这么说，教了我儿子打鼓。

00:40:34.400 --> 00:40:36.440
And it turned out he's great at it.
事实证明他打得很棒。

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:38.079
Music is so magical.
音乐真是太神奇了。

00:40:38.079 --> 00:40:39.400
I don't know that it can be studied.
我不知道这能不能被研究。

00:40:39.400 --> 00:40:40.920
I don't know if it can be decoded.
我不知道这能不能被破译。

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:49.920
why can my son, and I hope you're hearing this, Elliot, play pre-note perfect to stay away to heaven, but n***a button is kind of impossible.
为什么我的儿子——我希望你能听到这个，埃利奥特——能完美地弹奏出《通往天堂的阶梯》里的前奏音符，但要按错音却几乎是不可能的。

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:56.960
And you go, John Bonham, you can do John Bonham, but writing your hand writing's hard.
然后你会说，约翰·博纳姆，你可以成为约翰·博纳姆，但要写好你的字却很难。

00:40:56.960 --> 00:40:59.199
He's more of a typist than a hand writer.
比起手写，他更像是个打字员。

00:40:59.199 --> 00:41:01.119
And it's just a typical learner.
他只是个典型的学习者。

00:41:01.119 --> 00:41:04.880
A typical learner, that's another pretty accurate term, at least for Elliot.
一个典型的学习者，这也是个相当贴切的词，至少对埃利奥特来说是这样。

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:07.280
And it's just been wonderful.
这实在是太棒了。

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:13.360
So Audra, the mother of Max, She was one of my first readers of the manuscript when it was still just a manuscript.
奥德拉，也就是马克斯的母亲，她是我书稿最早的读者之一，当时这还只是一份书稿。

00:41:13.360 --> 00:41:14.519
It was unsold.
还没卖出去。

00:41:14.519 --> 00:41:17.159
And she was wonderful to take the time to read through it.
她非常乐意花时间把它读完。

00:41:17.159 --> 00:41:26.280
And as the mother of a kid with Down Syndrome and a kid who I really knew pretty well, Max, she gave me some great feedback to make sure it was specific and right to Down Syndrome.
作为唐氏综合征孩子的母亲，同时她也相当了解马克斯，她给了我一些极好的反馈，以确保书中关于唐氏综合征的描写足够具体和准确。

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:31.320
But she also knows Elliot and she knows that there's so many similarities between Elliot and Max.
但她也认识埃利奥特，她知道埃利奥特和马克斯之间有太多的相似之处。

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:36.280
And she was a good cheerleader of like, you're getting it right.
她一直是个很好的啦啦队长，鼓励我说：“你写得很对。”

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:45.480
But don't doubt yourself too much but maybe I would tweak these things and she gave me some really good specific details that made it into the book, so I'm very grateful for that.
“但也不要太自我怀疑，或许我可以微调一下这些地方，”她给了我一些非常好的具体细节，这些细节最终被写进了书中，对此我非常感激。

00:41:45.480 --> 00:41:52.679
At Tamara Beaver says, this book was so good, we have a lot of people writing in who have read the book and thank you to all of you and all the amazing things that you're saying.
塔马拉·比弗说：“这本书太棒了。”有很多读过这本书的人写信给我们，感谢你们所有人的到来，以及你们所说的那些赞美之词。

00:41:52.679 --> 00:41:53.639
Yeah, thank you.
是的，谢谢你们。

00:41:53.639 --> 00:41:56.119
This book was so good, hilarious, real, down to earth.
这本书太棒了，超级搞笑，真实，接地气。

00:41:56.119 --> 00:42:03.039
I love the multiple perspectives and as a speech language pathologist, love the disability representation.
我喜欢多重视角，作为一个语言病理学家，我非常喜欢书中的残障群体代表性。

00:42:03.039 --> 00:42:04.360
That's great.
这太棒了。

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:11.239
My son has had many wonderful speech therapists and I have nothing but huge respect.
我儿子遇到过许多出色的言语治疗师，我对他们只有无限的敬意。

00:42:11.239 --> 00:42:12.519
That's the thing on this journey.
这就是这段旅程的奇妙之处。

00:42:12.519 --> 00:42:16.199
You meet the most amazing people, occupational therapists, physical therapists, speech and language.
你会遇到最了不起的人：职业治疗师、物理治疗师、言语和语言治疗师。

00:42:16.199 --> 00:42:26.400
You meet these people who have devoted their lives to helping your kid and using creativity and patience and skill and just talent to help your child.
你会遇到这些人，他们将自己的一生奉献于帮助你的孩子，用创造力、耐心、专业技能和纯粹的天赋来帮助你的孩子。

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:31.320
And it really is, it's people you never would have met and there's so many hidden gifts on this journey.
这真的是……你会遇到那些平时根本不会遇到的人，这段旅程中有那么多隐藏的礼物。

00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:37.000
But yeah, sorry, I'll ask the question, but I just so much respect for you for doing that.
但是，好吧，抱歉，我要问问题了，但我对你们做这些事情充满敬意。

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:38.679
Thank you on behalf of parents like me.
我谨代表像我这样的父母向你们表示感谢。

00:42:38.679 --> 00:42:39.880
My father studied.
我父亲曾学过。

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:44.039
I think he actually might not have had a degree in speech in speech language.
我想他可能甚至没有获得言语或言语语言学学位。

00:42:44.039 --> 00:42:45.039
I'm not even saying the right words.
我甚至连词都说不对了。

00:42:45.039 --> 00:42:47.519
Something with speech and with language, with therapy.
总之是关于言语、语言和治疗的某些东西。

00:42:47.519 --> 00:42:48.440
I don't have it right.
我说得不对。

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:49.800
And then he became an accountant.
后来他成了一名会计。

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:58.119
So I also want people to be very crystal clear that this book is fucking hysterical.
所以我也想让大家非常清楚地知道，这本书简直笑死人了。

00:42:58.119 --> 00:43:23.880
And that as himian fans, which I assume everyone listening to this probably is, The similarities are there in your lack of attention span, perhaps I could say it that way, that you don't, are not attracted to as a writer, it would seem the straight and narrow narrative is beginning, middle, and end.
作为黑米安（Hemian）的粉丝——我猜听这个节目的每个人大概都是——你们的相似之处在于缺乏专注力，也许我可以说，你们作为作家，似乎并不喜欢那种规规矩矩、有头有尾的直线叙事。

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:24.719
Yeah.
是的。

00:43:24.719 --> 00:43:26.119
So let's get around a lot.
所以我们常常跳来跳去。

00:43:26.119 --> 00:43:31.199
It does, let's connect the book in terms of style and substance a little bit to the show.
确实如此，让我们把这本书在风格和内容上与这部剧稍微联系起来。

00:43:31.199 --> 00:43:46.980
Now on the podcast, I was just editing episode 14, coming out in a couple of weeks, And it said, he said, Josh said, I like when the narrator comes in at the end when Bob's second comes in, old Ted comes in at the end and says, and that's how dot, dot, dot, right?
在播客里，我刚刚在剪辑几周后要播出的第14集，里面说到，乔什说，我喜欢那种当旁白在结尾出现时，当鲍勃第二出现时，老特德在结尾出现并说：“事情就是这样，省略号，对吧？”

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:48.820
Some sort of lesson.
某种教训。

00:43:48.820 --> 00:43:51.340
And Josh said, because this isn't just how I met your mother.
乔什说，因为这不仅仅是《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:55.739
And you said, right, it's how a lot of shit happened.
然后你说，没错，这只是一堆破事发生的过程。

00:43:55.739 --> 00:43:58.659
And the name of your book is, that's not how it happened.
而你书的名字就是《事情可不是这样的》。

00:43:58.659 --> 00:44:02.380
So you definitely have an interest, obviously.
所以你显然对此很感兴趣。

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:05.139
Yeah, I really like the word how, apparently.
对，我显然很喜欢“如何”（How）这个词。

00:44:05.139 --> 00:44:06.139
That's what we've learned.
这就是我们所了解到的。

00:44:06.139 --> 00:44:07.139
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:44:07.139 --> 00:44:13.579
So there's clearly connection in your brain with these projects now, but you and I grew up watching very like down the line storytelling, right?
所以你的大脑里显然将这些项目联系在了一起，但你和我成长过程中看的是那种非常传统的故事讲述方式，对吧？

00:44:13.579 --> 00:44:16.340
Like mash, cheers, sort of that simple thing.
比如《陆军流动医院》、《干杯Cheers》，那种简单的东西。

00:44:16.340 --> 00:44:24.099
Then, you know, Seinfeld started to get a little crazy, Larry Sanders, and then Quentin Tarantino sort of blew all our minds, right?
然后呢，你知道的，宋飞开始变得有点疯狂，拉里·桑德斯，接着昆汀·塔伦蒂诺彻底震撼了我们，对吧？

00:44:24.099 --> 00:44:26.468
I was actually thinking, Craig, also that J.D.
其实我在想，克雷格，还有J.D.

00:44:26.468 --> 00:44:39.699
Salinger, which who we love, that in the later books, after a catcher, when he sort of piecing together the glass family, deep cut for everyone to go listen to these, It's also, it's all these little mini mysteries and you're going back and forward in time.
塞林格——我们都很喜欢他——在后来的书里，比如《麦田里的守望者》之后，当他把格拉斯家族的故事一点点拼凑起来时，这对每个人来说都是个冷门佳作，大家真该去听听这些，里面全是一个个小小的迷你悬念，时间线也在过去和未来之间穿梭。

00:44:39.699 --> 00:44:42.539
So I know that all inspired you.
所以我知道这些都给了你灵感。

00:44:42.539 --> 00:44:45.420
This book is not a straight narrative start to finish.
这本书并不是一条从头到尾的直线叙事。

00:44:45.420 --> 00:44:57.860
Can you inspire our readers, our listeners a little bit into the other reason why they're gonna wanna read this book is structurally, it's fucking awesome and not un-himium-like.
你能否稍微激发一下我们的读者和听众的兴趣，让他们知道为什么要读这本书的另一个原因：它的结构简直他妈的太棒了，而且很有“老爸老妈浪漫史”的风格。

00:44:57.860 --> 00:45:00.460
Yeah, and I feel like there's a lot of overlaps to him.
对，我觉得它和那部剧有很多重合之处。

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:04.579
And to talk about the comedy piece, and I'll get back to that, the structural piece.
说到喜剧元素——我一会儿会回过头来讲——还有结构本身。

00:45:06.519 --> 00:45:17.519
Books about disability are, and I talk about this in my book about disability, I talk about books about disability, they're often just inspiring, they're supposed to be inspiring and feel good.
关于残障题材的书籍往往……我在我的残障题材书中也探讨过这一点，我谈到过关于残障的书籍，它们通常只是为了励志，本意是应该鼓舞人心、让人感觉温馨。

00:45:17.519 --> 00:45:22.599
And then there's this other genre about disability where it's supposed to be very dark and we show how hard it is.
但还有另一类残障题材的小说，它们旨在表现得非常阴暗，向我们展示生活的艰辛。

00:45:22.599 --> 00:45:26.800
And I think what I was going for in my book is I wanted it to be funny.
我想在我的书中追求的是，我希望它是有趣的。

00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:30.679
There just aren't that many books that are funny, that are about this topic.
市面上讲这个主题却又妙趣横生的书实在不多。

00:45:30.679 --> 00:45:35.000
And I thought, I don't want to do inspiration porn, and I don't want to do torture porn.
我想过，我既不想搞“励志色情”，也不想搞“苦难色情”。

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:44.480
I want it to be, these are human beings, and life is funny, and believe me, the disability community, and the ones, the people who love them and their parents, we need to laugh.
我希望它是：这些都是活生生的人，生活充满笑料；相信我，残障群体，以及那些爱着他们的人和他们的父母，我们需要欢笑。

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:46.679
We have to laugh all the time.
我们必须无时无刻不在笑。

00:45:46.679 --> 00:45:48.559
You have to find a way to laugh.
你必须找到一种欢笑的方式。

00:45:48.559 --> 00:45:52.599
And that was my absolute top goal in this book, was for it to be funny.
这也是我写这本书的绝对首要目标，那就是要有趣。

00:45:53.519 --> 00:45:57.239
Second most was, what's a fun structure to tell a story?
排在第二位的是，讲述一个故事有哪些好玩的结构？

00:45:57.239 --> 00:46:04.599
How can I make sure I'm jumping around and kind of, how can a book have the editing style of how I met your mother?
我该如何确保情节不断跳跃，怎么说呢，一本书如何能拥有《老爸老妈浪漫史》那种剪辑风格？

00:46:04.599 --> 00:46:09.469
Which, just, boredom not allowed.
也就是，绝对不允许无聊。

00:46:09.469 --> 00:46:10.949
We were allergic to boredom.
我们对无聊过敏。

00:46:10.949 --> 00:46:12.750
We must never get bored.
我们绝不能感到无聊。

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:20.989
And it was interesting writing it, because I thought, I'm going to write it in such a way that if I start to get bored, I can know that I've gone past the end point of that section.
当时写作的过程很有意思，因为我想：我要这样写——如果我开始感到无聊了，那就说明我已经把那个小节写过头了。

00:46:20.989 --> 00:46:23.510
And I have to challenge myself, no, this little section.
我必须挑战自己，不，这个小节到此为止。

00:46:23.510 --> 00:46:33.949
And the book is really written largely in little four or five, six, maybe eight, 10 page sections, switching points of view, I really challenge myself, like, if I'm getting bored, the reader's getting bored.
这本书确实在很大程度上是由一个个四、五、六，或者八、十页的小节组成的，不断切换视角。我真的在挑战自己：如果我都觉得无聊了，读者也一定会觉得无聊。

00:46:33.949 --> 00:46:35.630
We have to end this here.
我们必须在这里结束。

00:46:35.630 --> 00:46:42.949
And I really wanted to end as many sections as I could, even within chapters, on cliffhangers, because that's the how of it.
而且我真的希望尽可能多的章节——甚至是章节内部的小节——都能以悬念结尾，因为这就是“如何”的秘诀所在。

00:46:42.949 --> 00:46:44.550
How means mystery?
“如何”意味着悬念？

00:46:44.550 --> 00:46:46.070
The word how to me equals mystery.
对我来说，“如何”这个词就等于悬念。

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:46.909
How did this happen?
这是怎么发生的？

00:46:46.909 --> 00:46:47.869
How do we solve the mystery?
我们如何解开这个谜团？

00:46:47.869 --> 00:46:52.110
All the best How Much Mother episodes are many mysteries within the larger mystery.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》里所有最棒的剧集，都是宏大谜团之下的一个个小谜团。

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:56.909
And this book has this larger mystery of, what's gonna happen to this family?
而这本书也有一个更大的悬念：这个家庭会怎么样？

00:46:56.909 --> 00:46:58.269
How is this movie gonna come together?
这部电影能顺利拍成吗？

00:46:58.269 --> 00:47:06.783
is this movie being made of the family going to destroy the family, how are all of these characters going to do on this cliff they're standing on?
这部关于这个家庭的电影会毁了这个家庭吗？这些角色站在悬崖边上，最终会落得什么下场？

00:47:06.783 --> 00:47:11.681
I use this metaphor that so many parents of kids with disabilities use of the cliff.
我用了这样一个隐喻，许多残障孩子的父母都用过“悬崖”这个比喻。

00:47:11.681 --> 00:47:19.320
When you get to around age 22, all of the education and supports and services that your child has had since literally one year old.
当你到了22岁左右的时候，孩子从一岁起就享受的所有教育、支持和福利，全都会戛然而止。

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:24.510
And that's when that and that moment is called the cliff within the disability community.
在残障群体中，那个时刻被称为“悬崖”。

00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:33.429
the cliff you fall off of, and we all spend, it's the thing that keeps parents like me up at night, and we don't have enough good answers as a society.
也就是你坠落的悬崖，我们所有人都在面对它，这是让像我这样的父母夜不能寐的事，而作为社会，我们并没有足够的完美答案。

00:47:33.429 --> 00:47:34.269
We do not.
确实没有。

00:47:34.269 --> 00:47:42.949
America, we don't have enough good answers to this, and this is a family, Paige is a mom, who creates an answer for Emmett by creating this business.
美国，我们对此没有足够的满分答案。而佩吉是一位母亲，她通过创立这样一家企业，为埃米特找到了一个答案。

00:47:42.949 --> 00:47:47.630
That's a cafe that he can work at with all employees that have different types of disabilities.
那是一家咖啡馆，他可以在那里工作，所有的员工都患有不同类型的残障。

00:47:47.630 --> 00:47:52.429
This is based on another amazing human I met in this amazing journey I've been on for 18 years.
这原型来自于我在过去18年这段奇妙旅程中遇到的另一个了不起的人。

00:47:52.429 --> 00:48:03.030
A woman up in the Berkshires and her husband that they founded, I hope you give them a shout out, extra special teas, it's a tea house, there's two of them in the Berkshires in Massachusetts and it's amazing.
伯克希尔山脉的一位女士和她的丈夫，我希望你能帮他们宣传一下，叫“格外特别茶饮”，是一家茶馆，在马萨诸塞州的伯克希尔有两家，非常棒。

00:48:03.030 --> 00:48:05.550
And basically that's cafe everyone in my book.
基本上，这就是我书中的“人人咖啡馆”。

00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:18.050
I basically met them, interviewed them, I said, I wanna take this, I don't wanna take what you've done and use it in my book and they were just so welcoming to me and they gave me, they shared their life with me and that's what made it into the book.
我基本上是去见了他们，采访了他们，然后我说：我想把这个……我想把你们做的事借鉴到我的书里。他们对我都非常欢迎，向我敞开心扉，分享了他们的生活，这些最终都写进了书中。

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:24.188
And yeah, I mean, it's basically, I wanted to tell a story where I could be spinning that many plates.
真的，我的意思是，基本上，我想讲一个能够同时转动这么多盘子的故事。

00:48:24.188 --> 00:48:28.340
Even as I'm answering this question, there's so many plates spinning.
即使在回答这个问题的同时，也有这么多的盘子在转动。

00:48:28.340 --> 00:48:34.418
I wanted to be able to like how I met your mother, be approaching story from a lot of angles at once.
我想像《老爸老妈浪漫史》那样，同时从多个角度来推进故事。

00:48:34.418 --> 00:48:38.812
And if any plate stops spinning, you had the next one to spin after that.
如果任何一个盘子停止转动，你还有下一个盘子可以接着转。

00:48:38.812 --> 00:48:43.867
And it was really fun to write with these four perspectives, and I didn't get bored.
用这四个视角来写作真的很有趣，而且我一点也不觉得枯燥。

00:48:43.867 --> 00:48:44.890
It was wonderful.
这感觉太美妙了。

00:48:44.890 --> 00:48:58.189
It reminded me of writing how much much mother, but I will say this, because it is structurally complicated, I needed to put it up on a dry erase board, just like in the writer's room of how much mother quite a few times.
这让我想起了创作《老爸老妈浪漫史》的时光，但我得承认，因为它的结构很复杂，我不得不像当年在《老爸老妈浪漫史》的编剧室里那样，好多次把它们画在白板上。

00:48:58.190 --> 00:49:01.710
Whenever I got stuck, I said, we're going back to old school, how much mother rules.
每当我卡壳的时候，我就会说：我们得回归老派的《老爸老妈浪漫史》法则了。

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:03.050
Time to get the dry erase markers.
是时候拿出白板笔了。

00:49:03.050 --> 00:49:07.389
Every character's story had a color, purple is Emma, green is Paige.
每个角色的故事都有专属的颜色，紫色是埃姆，绿色是佩吉。

00:49:07.389 --> 00:49:11.349
I'm going to do it just like we broke stories in the writer's room on the dry erase board.
我就像当年在编剧室的白板上构思故事那样去做。

00:49:11.349 --> 00:49:16.380
Whenever I got stuck, that's what I did.
每当我遇到瓶颈，我就是这么做的。

00:49:16.380 --> 00:49:24.340
And I know you pretty well and have known you for a long time.
我其实挺了解你的，认识你也很长时间了。

00:49:24.340 --> 00:49:31.610
And I was grateful to learn more stuff about you.
我很感激能通过这本书了解你更多的事。

00:49:31.610 --> 00:49:34.010
And then I also had to try to pause and go, hold on a second.
然后我也不得不试着停下来想一想：等一下。

00:49:34.010 --> 00:49:35.010
I know he's also a writer.
我知道他也是个作家。

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:37.210
There's a lot of fiction in here.
这里面有很多虚构的成分。

00:49:37.210 --> 00:49:38.610
This is not a biography.
这不是一本传记。

00:49:38.610 --> 00:49:39.610
Right.
没错。

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:44.250
I tried to sort of, and then there were times where I could, you know, I could hear Rebecca's voice.
我试着去理清……有时候，我能听到丽贝卡的的声音。

00:49:44.250 --> 00:49:48.889
you know, I could tell Rebecca's voice was there or parts of her story.
你知道的，我能感觉到丽贝卡的声音或者她故事的一部分就在里面。

00:49:48.889 --> 00:49:55.880
Was there anything where you said, fuck, maybe I just shouldn't reveal this.
有没有哪个瞬间，你会对自己说：操，也许我不该把这个写出来？

00:49:55.880 --> 00:50:01.920
And you, I'm not gonna, well, I guess my question could be and didn't put it in the book, but then you wouldn't tell us about it.
而你——我不会……好吧，我的问题可能是，如果没写进书里，你就不会跟我们说起它了。

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:04.280
Was there anything that was like, none of the murders are in there.
有没有什么事情是这样的——比如，所有的谋杀案都没写进去。

00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:05.719
Okay, not one, not one.
好吧，一个也没有，一个都没有。

00:50:06.719 --> 00:50:08.519
That's why I so short.
所以书才会这么短。

00:50:08.519 --> 00:50:12.500
Were there, what was on the bubble, but that made it in any way.
有没有什么内容是曾在边缘摇摆，但最后还是被收录进去了？

00:50:12.500 --> 00:50:17.460
Many things, I guess, but so much of a disfictional.
我想有很多吧，但大部分都是虚构的。

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:18.980
The character is not my wife.
书里的这个角色并不是我妻子。

00:50:18.980 --> 00:50:26.900
There are aspects of my wife in there, but there are aspects of other people I've met along the way, some of whom I've even mentioned in this conversation, and some of it's just fiction.
里面有我妻子的影子，但也有我在一路上遇到其他人的影子，其中有些人我甚至在这场对话中提到过，还有一些纯粹是虚构的。

00:50:26.900 --> 00:50:31.739
So I felt pretty protected by the many different ingredients and all of these characters.
所以我觉得，通过将各种不同的元素揉进所有这些角色里，我得到了很好的保护。

00:50:31.739 --> 00:50:33.739
Here was the thing that made me feel the best.
最让我感到安心的是下面这一点。

00:50:33.739 --> 00:50:45.860
The character, whenever I would feel like, I has this too personal, am I stealing this from life, too much, what saved me is the character who's the closest to me in the story is by far the shittiest.
每当我感觉——这会不会太私人了？我是不是把现实生活抄得太多了？——每当这时，拯救我的设定是：在故事里跟我最相近的那个角色，绝对是最混蛋的。

00:50:47.179 --> 00:50:49.460
Rob, a great strong degree, by the way.
罗布，非常彻底的一个混蛋。

00:50:49.460 --> 00:50:52.380
All the worst things in this book are done by the guy closest to me.
书里所有最糟糕的事，都是那个跟我最亲近的家伙干的。

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:55.539
So I was like, I'm throwing myself the most under the bus.
所以我当时想：我这是把自己狠狠地推到车轮底下去碾啊。

00:50:55.539 --> 00:50:56.780
And again, Rob is not me.
再说一次，罗布并不代表我。

00:50:56.780 --> 00:50:59.739
I didn't do the things that Rob did in this book exactly.
我并没有完全做过罗布在书里做的那些事。

00:50:59.739 --> 00:51:09.300
But some of the elements of wondering, what was it like for my wife to have to be home while I got on to go off into this fantasy land and make it how I'm at your mother.
但某些元素确实存在，比如我会好奇：当我去追逐这个幻想世界、去搞《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，我妻子一个人留在家里的感受是怎样的？

00:51:09.300 --> 00:51:26.019
and these dynamics that form, yeah, I wanted to investigate that and really be honest and really hold those parts of myself to account and the narcissism of a middle-aged guy who is trying to, in his 50s, there's that ego death of maybe your career has cooled.
以及由此产生的那些家庭动力，是的，我想要去审视这些，做到真正地坦诚，真正去追究自己性格中那些部分的责任，去剖析一个中年男人的自恋——当他在50多岁时，可能会遭遇职业生涯冷却的“自我之死”。

00:51:26.019 --> 00:51:28.059
Who are you without career definition?
如果没有了职业光环，你到底是谁？

00:51:28.059 --> 00:51:30.260
I think that's another thing we don't know how to talk about.
我觉得这是另一个我们不知道该如何开口谈论的话题。

00:51:30.260 --> 00:51:37.420
We don't know how to talk about disability and we don't know how to talk about who are we, besides success, besides achievement, especially men, who are we?
我们不知道怎么谈论残障，也不知道怎么谈论除了成功和成就之外，我们究竟是谁——尤其是男人，我们到底是谁？

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:40.280
Like, who are we underneath the things we're chasing?
比如，在我们在不断追逐的事物背后，我们究竟是个什么样的人？

00:51:40.280 --> 00:51:49.199
And yeah, I always felt like when in doubt, make sure to come the hardest at the character closest to me.
所以是的，我始终觉得，如果心存疑虑，那就对离我最近的角色下手最狠。

00:51:49.199 --> 00:51:55.360
And when in doubt, the characters who bear some resemblance to the members of my family, make them the heroes.
如果心存疑虑，那就把那些与我家人的成员有几分相似的角色塑造成英雄。

00:51:55.360 --> 00:51:59.679
But, and that's not, I'm not doing that just not to get yelled at.
但这并不是为了不挨骂才这么做的。

00:51:59.679 --> 00:52:03.760
I'm doing that because it's the most interesting to me.
我这么做是因为这对我来说最有吸引力。

00:52:03.760 --> 00:52:04.840
It's the most interesting to me.
这对我而言最有趣。

00:52:04.840 --> 00:52:10.960
And there's a wonderful exercise in saying, what's wonderful about these people that I love?
去探讨“我爱的人身上有什么美好的特质”，这是一种绝佳的创作练习。

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:12.559
How do I show them have flaws?
我该如何展现他们的缺点？

00:52:12.559 --> 00:52:25.719
But how do I show that the wonderfulness transcends and makes it through in the end, and shines through the brightest in the end, and even for Rob in the end, who experiences redemption at the end of this book.
但我又该如何证明，那些美好的特质最终超越了一切并支撑着他们走过难关，在最后大放异彩？甚至对罗布来说也是如此，他在本书的结尾迎来了救赎。

00:52:25.719 --> 00:52:30.800
And even though he's had, he has in some ways the hardest time evolving of these four people.
尽管在这四个人当中，他在某些方面是最难完成蜕变的那一个。

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:32.599
And that's the big twist of the book.
这也是这本书最大的反转所在。

00:52:32.599 --> 00:52:38.320
We talk about the cliff when we talk about young adults with a disability, but every character in this book is on a cliff.
当我们谈论患有残障的年轻人时，我们总会提到“悬崖”，但事实上，这本书里的每一个角色都正站在悬崖边上。

00:52:38.320 --> 00:52:41.480
All of the characters are standing on some type of life cliff.
所有这些角色其实都站在某种生活的悬崖边上。

00:52:41.480 --> 00:52:46.679
And we all act like, ah, there's this one character that we have a label, there's this one person we have a label for.
而我们总是表现得好像，啊，有一个角色我们给贴上了标签，有一个人我们给贴上了标签。

00:52:46.679 --> 00:52:48.320
We know they have a disability.
我们知道他们有残疾。

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:52.320
We can say they're going to have challenges in life because we have a term we're putting on it.
我们可以断定他们在生活中会遇到挑战，因为我们给它安上了一个术语。

00:52:52.320 --> 00:52:55.119
No, we all have challenges.
不，我们所有人都有挑战。

00:52:55.119 --> 00:53:11.119
Whether there's a term or not to put on our own version of our challenges, whether it's considered a disability or not, we are all just as fragile and just as strong as anybody else, we're all the same and everybody is on some type of cliff.
不管有没有一个术语来定义我们各自版本的挑战，不管它是否被视为残疾，我们其实都和其他人一样脆弱，也一样坚强，我们都是一样的，每个人都处在某种悬崖边上。

00:53:11.119 --> 00:53:22.639
And once we realize that, maybe then we know how to talk about disability, which is to say we are all working on challenges and we are all people first and no label can explain anybody away.
一旦我们意识到这一点，也许我们就会知道该如何谈论残疾，那就是：我们都在应对各自的挑战，我们首先都是人，没有任何标签可以把任何人轻描淡写地排除在外。

00:53:22.639 --> 00:53:41.070
We're all sitting there on in some sort of, and at some sort of inflection point in our life, we all have these moments in our life where we have to figure shit out and evolve, and it doesn't matter what terminology or diagnosis we put on ourselves, we're all the same, we all have the same challenges, really.
我们都坐在某种地方，处于人生某种转折点上，我们的生活中都有这样一些时刻，我们必须搞清楚状况并完成蜕变，无论我们给自己贴上什么术语或诊断标签，我们其实都是一样的，真的，我们都面临着同样的挑战。

00:53:41.070 --> 00:53:44.710
All right, everyone, that is our Instagram live for today.
好了，各位，这就是我们今天的 Instagram 直播。

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:49.869
We've got, that's not how it happened, it's available, of course, everywhere.
我们的书《事情可不是这样的》当然在各大平台上都能买到。

00:53:49.869 --> 00:53:56.909
And we have a winner of our, winner's a strong word, golden globes don't do winter anymore, right?
我们还有一位——“赢家”这个词有点重了，金球奖现在也不分冬天了吧，对吧？

00:53:56.909 --> 00:54:01.150
It's the gold, the award goes to- I'm going to go to Golden Globe, didn't you hear?
是金，这个奖归——我要去金球奖了，你没听说吗？

00:54:01.150 --> 00:54:01.989
I heard earlier.
我刚才听说了。

00:54:01.989 --> 00:54:02.949
Yeah.
对。

00:54:02.949 --> 00:54:13.829
Well, the book, the autograph book, and we're going to figure out how to get it to you, is going to go to at Michelle McNeil 009 for just a series of fantastic questions.
那么这本书，这本签名书——我们会想办法把它交到你手上——将送给 @MichelleMcNeil009，因为她提出了一系列精彩绝伦的问题。

00:54:13.829 --> 00:54:15.590
And so thank you.
所以非常感谢。

00:54:15.590 --> 00:54:21.269
And we're going to figure out how to find you, and we don't know how to do these things.
我们会想办法找到你，虽然我们其实并不知道该怎么做这种事。

00:54:21.269 --> 00:54:23.989
Thank you all for suffering through our first attempt at this.
谢谢大家忍受我们第一次尝试这种形式。

00:54:23.989 --> 00:54:27.550
But more importantly, thanks everybody for joining us and for the awesome questions.
但更重要的是，谢谢大家加入我们，并提出了这么棒的问题。

00:54:27.550 --> 00:54:35.949
And yeah, just for having an interest in this book that means so much to me, there is a lot of overlap to how I met your mother in so many different ways.
还有，谢谢大家对这本书表现出的兴趣，它对我有极其重要的意义，它在很多不同方面与《老爸老妈浪漫史》有着很多重合之处。

00:54:35.949 --> 00:54:40.710
But it's also its own thing and it's also like, it's my favorite thing I've written besides how I met your mother.
但它也是一部独立的作品，而且就像是，除了《老爸老妈浪漫史》之外，它是我写过的最喜欢的东西。

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:41.710
So thank you.
所以谢谢大家。

00:54:41.710 --> 00:54:51.719
I'm so happy to be able to share this with you and the whole Hymnium community who have been so welcoming and supportive about this book in a way that just means the world to me.
我很高兴能与你们以及整个 Hymnium 社区分享这一切，你们对这本书的欢迎和支持对我来说意义重大。

00:54:51.719 --> 00:54:53.159
Alright, thank you Craig.
好的，谢谢你，克雷格。

00:54:53.159 --> 00:54:54.159
Thank you everyone.
谢谢大家。

00:54:54.159 --> 00:54:55.159
Check out the book.
去看看这本书吧。

00:54:55.159 --> 00:55:02.760
of course check out How We Made Your Mother, wherever you get podcasts, tell your friends, and we will see you all again soon.
当然，也欢迎在各大播客平台收听《我们是如何促成你的母亲》的，告诉你的朋友们，我们很快就会再见到大家。

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:03.760
Thanks again.
再次感谢。

00:55:03.760 --> 00:55:04.760
Thanks everybody.
谢谢大家。

